<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <!--
      A model is gigabytes, so the download has to survive the app being backgrounded: that means
      a foreground worker, which means a notification, which means asking for one on API 33+.
      Refusing it does not stop the download - it only hides the progress.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_DATA_SYNC" />

    <!--
      The phone itself. Both optional in practice: without READ_CONTACTS a name cannot be looked
      up and the model has to be given a number, and without CALL_PHONE a call can only ever be
      staged in the dialer - which is what happens by default anyway. Neither is requested at
      launch; they are asked for at the point they are needed.
    -->
    <!--
      The photo library, for indexing it on the device so it can be searched by description.
      READ_MEDIA_IMAGES from API 33; the older permission is kept for 31 and 32, which this app
      still supports. VISUAL_USER_SELECTED means a partial grant on API 34+ works properly -
      the library query simply returns the photos the user picked, which is a reasonable answer
      rather than a failure.
    -->
    <!-- Dictation. Asked for at the microphone button, never at launch. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO" />

    <!--
      Holding the microphone open for the wake word.

      Its own foreground-service type, because that is what the system shows somebody when it
      tells them an app has the microphone. The service runs only while the setting is on.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MICROPHONE" />

    <!--
      Appearing over whatever is on screen when the wake word hears its name.

      This is not about drawing an overlay: Lin never does. It is the only exemption that lets
      an app in the background open a window at all. A foreground service is not one - the
      system drops the launch silently rather than refusing it - so without this, "Hey Lin"
      would hear the question, have nowhere to put it, and look broken. Granted by hand in the
      phone's own settings, like notification access, and optional: without it the wake word
      puts the question in a notification to tap instead, which is the one background start
      that is always allowed.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW" />

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_MEDIA_IMAGES"
        android:minSdkVersion="33" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_MEDIA_VISUAL_USER_SELECTED"
        android:minSdkVersion="34" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
        android:maxSdkVersion="32" />

    <!--
      Reading the calendar, to answer "what is on today".

      Read only. There is deliberately no WRITE_CALENDAR: an event Lin creates goes through the
      calendar app's own editor with the fields filled in, so a model that misreads a date
      cannot put anything in somebody's week - the worst it can do is open an editor showing
      the wrong day, which is visible before it is saved.
    -->
    <!--
      Where to look the weather up, and nothing else.

      Coarse rather than fine: a forecast is the same across a town, and the coordinates are
      rounded to about a kilometre again before they leave the phone. Lin asks for this only
      when somebody asks about the weather, and the only thing it is ever used for is that
      lookup - there is no background location, no history kept, and no other request this
      permission reaches.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION" />

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_CALENDAR" />

    <!-- Setting a timer outright. An alarm still opens the clock app to be confirmed. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="com.android.alarm.permission.SET_ALARM" />

    <!--
      Lin's own reminders, which are not the clock app's alarms.

      SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM rather than USE_EXACT_ALARM: the first is asked for and can be
      refused, the second is granted at install and cannot. A reminder is worth asking for and
      not worth taking - refused, ReminderBook falls back to an inexact alarm and the tool says
      the notification may be a few minutes late, which is a far better outcome than an app
      that quietly helps itself to a permission about punctuality.

      RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED is not optional for this feature. Alarms are lost on restart, so
      without it every reminder set before a reboot would be listed as pending and never arrive.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED" />

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_CONTACTS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CALL_PHONE" />

    <!--
      Borrowing the ADB shell's privileges through Shizuku, which is what makes "turn the
      wi-fi off" turn the wi-fi off instead of opening a panel for the user to tap.

      Not a system permission: Shizuku is a separate app the user installs and pairs once over
      Wireless debugging, and it lends its shell identity to apps the user allows one at a
      time. Declaring this only means Lin may ask; Shizuku's own prompt decides. Everything
      Lin does with it is in core/privileged, and all of it is a shell command a person could
      type themselves - svc wifi disable and the like - rather than a hidden system interface.

      Without Shizuku installed nothing here changes: the switches fall back to opening the
      phone's own panel, which is what they did before.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="moe.shizuku.manager.permission.API_V23" />

    <!--
      Needed to see that a dialer or a messaging app exists at all. Package visibility is
      restricted from API 30, and these are the two intents this app ever starts.
    -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.DIAL" />
            <data android:scheme="tel" />
        </intent>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.SENDTO" />
            <data android:scheme="smsto" />
        </intent>
        <!--
          Without these, startActivity for a calendar event or a timer throws
          ActivityNotFoundException on API 30 and up even when a calendar and a clock are
          installed - package visibility hides them, and the failure reads as "there is no
          calendar app on this phone".
        -->
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.INSERT" />
            <data android:mimeType="vnd.android.cursor.dir/event" />
        </intent>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.SET_ALARM" />
        </intent>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.SET_TIMER" />
        </intent>
        <!--
          Every app with an icon, which is what "open Instagram" needs and all it needs.

          Deliberately not QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES. That permission exists for app stores and
          antivirus; this one asks exactly the question Lin has - which apps could the user
          have opened themselves - and answers nothing else.
        -->
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
            <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
        </intent>
        <!--
          Apps that can play music, for the setting that picks one. Two ways of declaring it,
          because an app may answer either: a media browser service is what a car and a watch
          look for, and the play-from-search intent is what Lin asks with.
        -->
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.media.browse.MediaBrowserService" />
        </intent>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.media.action.MEDIA_PLAY_FROM_SEARCH" />
        </intent>
    </queries>

    <application
        android:name=".LinApplication"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/network_security_config"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Lin">

        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/app_name"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Lin"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize"
            android:configChanges="screenSize|smallestScreenSize|screenLayout|orientation|keyboardHidden">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
            <!--
              Sharing a document to Lin, which is the natural way to hand one over - from a
              mail attachment, a browser download, a file manager.

              Narrow on purpose. Only the three kinds this app can actually read are listed, so
              Lin does not appear in the share sheet for a photo or a video and then say it
              cannot read it. A share grants access to that one file and nothing around it.
            -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SEND" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <data android:mimeType="application/pdf" />
                <data android:mimeType="application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document" />
                <data android:mimeType="text/*" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
          How a document Lin wrote gets out of Lin's own storage.

          Scoped to the single directory it writes into - see documents_paths.xml. A provider
          rooted at "." would let anything holding a granted URI read the memory database and
          the settings as well, which is not a trade worth making to save a line of XML.
        -->
        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.documents"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/documents_paths" />
        </provider>

        <!--
          The one line that stops a download killing the app.

          A model download runs in a WorkManager foreground worker, and on API 34+ asking to go
          foreground means naming a service type. WorkManager's own service - the one that
          actually calls startForeground - declares no type at all, because only the app knows
          what its workers do. So the type has to be merged in here, and if it is not,
          startForeground throws:

              IllegalArgumentException: foregroundServiceType 0x00000001 is not a subset of
              foregroundServiceType attribute 0x00000000 in service element of manifest file

          WorkManager catches ForegroundServiceStartNotAllowedException and SecurityException
          around that call and nothing else, so this one falls through, on the main thread of the
          service, and takes the process with it. From the outside that is "the app crashes the
          moment I press Download" - and because the crash leaves the work record in a state
          WorkManager never finished, every later tap did nothing at all.

          dataSync is the right type: there is no download type, and this is a long transfer the
          user started and can see.
        -->
        <service
            android:name="androidx.work.impl.foreground.SystemForegroundService"
            android:foregroundServiceType="dataSync"
            tools:node="merge" />

        <!--
          Watches for messages that can be replied to. Only those - a notification with no
          reply action is ignored rather than collected, because this exists to answer messages
          and not to build a record of everything that crosses somebody's screen.

          Notification access is granted by the user in system settings and cannot be
          requested; the settings screen links there.
        -->
        <!--
          Lin as a panel over whatever you were doing, which is what being summoned should
          look like. A full-screen launch loses somebody's place and makes a ten-second
          question feel like leaving the app they were in.

          Its own activity rather than a mode of the main one, because the window's theme is
          fixed when it is created: a single activity reused for both would keep whichever
          window it was born with. singleTask so a second summon reuses this one rather than
          stacking panels, and excludeFromRecents so a finished question does not sit in the
          task switcher pretending to be an open app.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".AssistActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/app_name"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Lin.Assist"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:excludeFromRecents="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize"
            android:configChanges="screenSize|smallestScreenSize|screenLayout|orientation|keyboardHidden">
            <!--
              What makes Lin selectable as the phone's digital assistant, and so what the power
              button and the assistant gesture summon. This is the whole point of the project
              stated in one intent filter.

              The simple ASSIST filter rather than a VoiceInteractionService: the full form
              brings an overlay session and "Hey Google"-style always-listening, which needs a
              wake-word model this app does not have and a microphone open continuously, which
              it has not earned. This gets the role and the summon; the rest is Phase 6's
              remaining half.
            -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.ASSIST" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
            </intent-filter>
            <!--
              The voice button on a car kit or a headset, over Bluetooth.

              Not Android Auto's microphone button: that one goes to Assistant or Gemini and
              is not open to other apps. This is the older, simpler path - a Bluetooth
              hands-free unit's voice-dial button sends VOICE_COMMAND to the phone, and
              whichever app answers it handles the request. Which cars send it is a property
              of the head unit rather than of this app, so it is offered and not promised.
            -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VOICE_COMMAND" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
            </intent-filter>
            <!--
              The other thing a head unit or headset sends when its voice button is pressed.
              Android's own name for it is "voice search, hands free" - meant for a prompt
              that needs neither the user's eyes nor their hands, which is exactly the panel.
              Offered alongside VOICE_COMMAND because which of the two arrives belongs to the
              car rather than to this app.
            -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.speech.action.VOICE_SEARCH_HANDS_FREE" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
          A second icon that opens Lin already listening.

          This is the Galaxy integration that actually exists. Samsung's Modes and Routines
          cannot send an intent to an app and has no third-party trigger API, but one of the
          actions it does have is "open app" - and Routines+ adds double-press and long-press
          of the volume and side keys as triggers. An app with a second launcher entry is
          therefore reachable from a hardware button without Lin being the assistant at all,
          which is the thing the assistant role kept failing to deliver.

          It is also what the home screen, a folder, an edge panel's app shortcuts and a
          Bixby "open" all target, so one alias serves every one of them.

          An alias rather than another activity: it is the same panel, launched the same way.
          And because a routine opening an app is a visible, user-started launch, the
          microphone may start - which it may not when something tries this from the
          background.
        -->
        <activity-alias
            android:name=".ListenActivity"
            android:targetActivity=".AssistActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/listen_label"
            android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity-alias>

        <!--
          How Shizuku finds this app.

          Required by Shizuku rather than by Lin: its server looks this provider up to work out
          which app is asking and to hand back the binder. It is protected by
          INTERACT_ACROSS_USERS_FULL, which no ordinary app holds, so nothing but the system
          and Shizuku itself can reach it.
        -->
        <provider
            android:name="rikka.shizuku.ShizukuProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.shizuku"
            android:multiprocess="false"
            android:enabled="true"
            android:exported="true"
            android:permission="android.permission.INTERACT_ACROSS_USERS_FULL" />

        <!--
          Lin's hands, and its eyes for what is on screen.

          This is the heaviest privilege on Android and the manifest should say so plainly: a
          bound accessibility service can read the contents of every screen the person looks
          at and act on their behalf. It is what lets Lin write a message in WhatsApp's own
          composer - which no public API allows at all - and read out what is on the screen
          behind the panel.

          It is granted by hand, behind the system's own warning screen, and revocable in two
          taps. Lin asks for the narrowest form of it: window content, view ids, and nothing
          else. See res/xml/accessibility_service.xml for what is and is not requested, and
          LinAccessibility for why its event handler is deliberately empty.
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".screen.LinAccessibility"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/accessibility_label"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_ACCESSIBILITY_SERVICE">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.accessibilityservice.AccessibilityService" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.accessibilityservice"
                android:resource="@xml/accessibility_service" />
        </service>

        <!--
          Lin on the car's screen.

          Exported because the Android Auto host - a different app - binds it; LinCarService
          checks who is calling, and in a release build accepts only the hosts the Car App
          Library ships signatures for.

          The category is IOT, which needs explaining. Android Auto only shows templated apps
          in the categories it supports - navigation, points of interest, internet of things
          and weather - and an assistant is none of them. IOT is the closest true description:
          Lin's car screen is one control surface for things on the phone. Anything else here
          would be a claim the app cannot back up.
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".car.LinCarService"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/app_name"
            android:icon="@drawable/ic_notification">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="androidx.car.app.CarAppService" />
                <category android:name="androidx.car.app.category.IOT" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

        <!--
          What makes Android Auto list Lin at all. The car host looks for this entry before it
          looks for the service above; without it the service is never bound and Lin does not
          appear on the car's screen.
        -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="com.google.android.gms.car.application"
            android:resource="@xml/automotive_app_desc" />

        <!-- A reply spoken in Android Auto, arriving as text. Only Lin's own notification can send it. -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".car.CarChatReceiver"
            android:exported="false" />

        <!--
          Lin on the watch: Wear OS wakes this when the watch app sends a question. Exported
          because Google Play services delivers the message; it only accepts Lin's own path.
        -->
        <!-- Home screen widgets: "Ask Lin", and Lin's latest answer. -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".widget.AskWidgetReceiver"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/widget_ask_label">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.appwidget.action.APPWIDGET_UPDATE" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data android:name="android.appwidget.provider" android:resource="@xml/widget_ask" />
        </receiver>
        <receiver
            android:name=".widget.LastAnswerWidgetReceiver"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/widget_answer_label">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.appwidget.action.APPWIDGET_UPDATE" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data android:name="android.appwidget.provider" android:resource="@xml/widget_answer" />
        </receiver>

        <!-- A voice conversation carrying on after Lin leaves the screen. See VoiceSession. -->
        <service
            android:name=".voice.VoiceSessionService"
            android:exported="false"
            android:foregroundServiceType="microphone" />

        <service
            android:name=".wear.WearBridge"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="com.google.android.gms.wearable.MESSAGE_RECEIVED" />
                <data android:scheme="wear" android:host="*" android:pathPrefix="/lin" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

        <!-- The oldest car host Lin can talk to. Level 6 is what the IOT category needs. -->
        <meta-data android:name="androidx.car.app.minCarApiLevel" android:value="6" />

        <!--
          A reminder coming due, and the restart that would otherwise have lost it.

          Not exported: the only things that may send to it are the alarm manager and the
          system's boot broadcast, both of which reach a receiver in the app's own package.
          MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED as well as BOOT_COMPLETED, because an update clears alarms
          exactly as a reboot does.

          Not direct-boot aware, and so deliberately not listening for
          LOCKED_BOOT_COMPLETED: the reminders are kept in the app's own encrypted storage,
          which cannot be read until the phone is first unlocked. Reminders come back then,
          which is also the first moment anybody could see a notification.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".remind.ReminderReceiver"
            android:exported="false">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="com.lin.agent.REMIND" />
            </intent-filter>
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED" />
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>

        <!-- Listens for "Hey Lin". Started by the setting, and stopped with it. -->
        <service
            android:name=".voice.WakeService"
            android:exported="false"
            android:foregroundServiceType="microphone" />

        <service
            android:name=".notifications.LinNotificationListener"
            android:exported="false"
            android:label="@string/app_name"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_NOTIFICATION_LISTENER_SERVICE">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.service.notification.NotificationListenerService" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

        <!--
          The vendor libraries the engine is allowed to load, and the reason the GPU works.

          Since Android 12 an app may only dlopen a vendor or system native library it declares
          here. Nothing else gates it: the library is on the device, the engine asks for it by
          name, and the linker refuses because this list did not mention it. The failure reads
          "Can not find OpenCL library on this device", which is why it was first taken at face
          value - the phone in question has OpenCL and runs these same models on the GPU in
          Google's own Gallery app, which declares exactly this list.

          libOpenCL      - the GPU path for LiteRT. Without it every model runs on the CPU.
          libvndksupport - what lets a vendor library be opened at all.
          libcdsprpc     - Qualcomm's compute DSP, which is the NPU path on Snapdragon.
          libedgetpu_*   - the same for Google Tensor.

          All optional: a device without one simply does not offer that accelerator, and the
          engine falls back. Declaring a library a device does not have costs nothing.
        -->
        <uses-native-library android:name="libvndksupport.so" android:required="false" />
        <uses-native-library android:name="libOpenCL.so" android:required="false" />
        <uses-native-library android:name="libcdsprpc.so" android:required="false" />
        <uses-native-library android:name="libedgetpu_litert.so" android:required="false" />
        <uses-native-library android:name="libedgetpu_util.so" android:required="false" />
        <uses-native-library android:name="libedgetpu_client.google.so" android:required="false" />
        <uses-native-library android:name="libedgetpu_tachyon.google.so" android:required="false" />
    </application>

</manifest>
